---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Leaderboard">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/tabulator-tables@6.3.0/dist/css/tabulator_simple.min.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <h1>Dip-Buy Leaderboard — bounce reliability at −10%</h1>
  <p>
    Given a <strong>−10% trigger</strong>, how reliably — and how fast — has each ticker
    recovered <strong>+10%</strong> before triggering another −10%? Ranked by the
    <strong>95% Wilson lower bound</strong> of the historical bounce rate, so thin samples
    are penalized automatically. This is a <em>base-rate display, not a prediction</em>:
    whether the ranking predicts anything at all is tested out-of-sample and reported in
    the banner below.
  </p>

  <p id="bullish-meta"><em>Loading screen…</em></p>

  <div id="validation-banner"></div>

  <div id="bullish-scatter" style="min-height:520px;"></div>

  <h2>Leaderboard</h2>

  <p style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
    Don't see a stock?
    <a href="https://github.com/sivadotblog/stock-threshold-analyzer/issues/new?template=add-ticker.md&title=Add+ticker%3A+XXXX&labels=add-ticker"
       target="_blank" rel="noopener"
       style="font-weight:600;">Open a GitHub issue to request it →</a>
  </p>

  <div id="tb-filters" class="tb-filter-bar">
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-ticker">Ticker</label>
      <input type="text" id="f-ticker" placeholder="e.g. RKLB" autocomplete="off" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-rank-max">Rank ≤</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-rank-max" placeholder="e.g. 100" min="1" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-wilson-min">Wilson low ≥</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-wilson-min" placeholder="e.g. 0.5" step="0.01" min="0" max="1" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label for="f-n-min">n events ≥</label>
      <input type="number" id="f-n-min" placeholder="e.g. 10" min="0" />
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group">
      <label>CAGR%</label>
      <div class="tb-filter-range">
        <input type="number" id="f-cagr-min" placeholder="min" />
        <span>–</span>
        <input type="number" id="f-cagr-max" placeholder="max" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tb-filter-group tb-filter-group--btn">
      <button id="f-reset">Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="bullish-table"></div>

  <div class="field-legend">
    <h2>Field Descriptions</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Signal</strong></td>
          <td>Current §5.5 state: <code>BUY_SETUP</code> (fresh −10% trigger on a top-K
          ticker, actionable at the <em>next open</em> — never same-day),
          <code>IN_TRADE</code>, <code>SELL_*</code> (mechanical exits), or
          <code>SUPPRESSED</code> (a signal would exist, but validation is WEAK/FAILED or
          the sample is too thin — greyed reason in the tooltip, never actionable).
          Hover a badge for trigger price, stop level, time-stop date and the historical
          base rate behind it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Wilson low</strong></td>
          <td>The ranking key: lower bound of the 95% Wilson confidence interval on the
          bounce rate. A ticker that bounced 5/5 ranks <em>below</em> one that bounced
          40/52 — small samples get less credit, replacing any hard eligibility cutoff.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Bounce rate</strong></td>
          <td>Of the −10% triggers that resolved, the fraction whose <em>next</em> event
          was the +10% recovery (a bounce) rather than another −10% drop (a
          continuation), shown with the raw counts. Unresolved (censored) triggers are
          excluded from the rate but counted in <strong>Cens</strong> — a ticker whose
          last dip never resolved is important information.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>n▼ / Cens</strong></td>
          <td>Number of −10% down-triggers in the 5-year window (the denominator — never
          hidden), and how many of them are still unresolved. ⚠️ marks
          <code>low_sample</code> rows (&lt;10 down events).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Med / p90 days</strong></td>
          <td>Median and 90th-percentile calendar days from a −10% trigger to its +10%
          recovery, over bounce-resolved events. p90 is the tail risk of capital being
          stuck.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Med / Worst MAE%</strong></td>
          <td>Max adverse excursion: how much further the price fell beyond the trigger
          before resolving. Expect a typical entry to get about the median MAE worse
          before it recovers; the stop is sized from this.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Expect%</strong></td>
          <td>Mean signed return per resolved trigger (bounces contribute ~+10%,
          continuations ~−10%), before costs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Streak</strong></td>
          <td>Current momentum. How many consecutive ±10% threshold crossings have gone the same direction. <code>+3↑</code> = the last 3 events were all up legs. <code>-2↓</code> = last 2 were down legs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Recent Δ</strong></td>
          <td>Every ±10% threshold crossing in the last 30 days, oldest to newest — e.g. <code>-1↓ -1↓ +1↑</code> for two down-legs then an up-leg. Based on the actual sequence of events, not calendar days, so it isn't affected by weekends/holidays and naturally shows more badges for an active stock and fewer for a quiet one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>v1 score (deprecated)</strong></td>
          <td>The old <code>bullish_score</code> composite (activity × trend). Kept greyed
          during the deprecation period for comparison only — it measured whether dips
          <em>already</em> resolved upward over the lookback (survivorship-shaped), not
          whether they will.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <script is:inline src="https://unpkg.com/tabulator-tables@6.3.0/dist/js/tabulator.min.js"></script>
  <script is:inline src={`${base}/js/bullish-screen.js`}></script>
</Base>
